An Irq is bound in a pool whose destroy has begun,
so a thread binding between the destroy's steps keeps it from ending.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call built an object in a pool being destroyed

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -516,9 +516,6 @@ static int op_irq_line(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
             return err;
         }
         struct pool *pool = (struct pool *)cap_object(&pc);
-        if (pool->dying) {
-            return KERR_STATE;
-        }
         /* The Irq signals on its creator's behalf, so the creator must be able to. */
         struct cap nc;
         err = cap_lookup_typed(thread_table(t), arg[2], CAP_NOTIFICATION, RIGHT_W, &nc);
